- -O2 tail calls: return f(args) -> jmp f. Still open for multiple arguments
  (needs the same argument-overlap care GCC's sibcall pass takes --
  see torture/20050713-1.c's own comment).

- -O2 performance: IR backend for eligible functions

  Problem. -O2 is currently as slow as -O0 (bench: 763 vs 786 ms; AWFY 6499 vs
  6504 ms), slower than TCC, ~4x behind gcc -O2 and antcc. Kernel timings (ms):
      kernel   rcc -O2   antcc   gcc -O0   gcc -O2
      sum        1217      929     3417       671
      axpy       2262      606     3615       234
      fib         431      323      393        82
      norm       5219     1209     2743       649
      mm         2062      376     1049       260
  On norm and mm rcc -O2 is slower than gcc -O0.

  Causes (all in the AST-walking codegen, not in opt.c):
  - hot-local promotion (codegen.c "-O2+: hot scalar locals") is 3 regs
    (rbx, r12, r13), int/long/ptr only, params excluded: no FP, no structs,
    loop bounds reloaded from the stack each iteration.
  - float/double values live in GPR vregs: every op is movq xmm<->gpr + mulsd.
  - no scaled-index addressing for a[i]/p->f (shl+movslq+add per access);
    no memory-operand ALU/FP forms in the asm layer.
  - loops test at the top plus a back jmp; ?: and if materialize setcc+movzx+cmp.
  - oversized frames for leaf functions, scalar returns round-trip the stack,
    "mov $0,%eax" before non-variadic calls.
  - -O3 == -O2 (the AST SSA rename pass is gone). CSE is a tiny ring buffer;
    ssa_unroll is disabled.
  - inliner only takes single-return-expression callees.

  Plan: Do not add more special cases to the
  tree codegen; lower eligible functions to a basic-block SSA IR instead:
    AST -> IR -> mem2reg (Braun et al., no dominance frontiers) -> IR passes
        -> linear-scan regalloc (loop-weighted spill cost, ABI fixed intervals,
           copy hints) -> isel with address folding -> existing x86_* encoder.
  - Per-function eligibility, fallback to the current codegen otherwise: scalar
    int/ptr/float/double, loads/stores, calls, structured control flow. Not
    eligible: VLA, asm, complex, int128/bitint/decimal/vector, setjmp, cleanup,
    nested fns, computed goto.
  - x86-64 first, then ARM64 (ir_arm64.c) and Windows/COFF (Win64 in ir_x86.c).

  Steps:
  3. MOSTLY DONE: IR inliner (small same-TU callees without frame slots,
     multi-block/multi-return bodies spliced before ir_opt; IR built for the
     whole TU up front so callees defined later inline too; templates are
     ir_opt'd once so the size caps see optimized code; honors
     __attribute__((noinline)), which the AST inliner now also honors;
     -fno-ir-inline; call-heavy 0.77s old / 0.21s no-inline / 0.13s inlined /
     gcc 0.14s). ir_opt: redundant-load elimination, store-to-load
     forwarding (exact-width + TRUNC/SEXT/ZEXT), dead stores, non-escaping
     frame-slot analysis. abs/labs/llabs lowered inline. Tail-recursive calls
     become loops (ir_tailrec in ir_inline.c; weak/aliased/interposable
     self-calls kept as calls). IR frames sized from surviving FRAMEADDRs
     (AST stack_size counted slots SSA had promoted). Noreturn calls
     (__attribute__((noreturn)) / _Noreturn / [[noreturn]]; glibc
     abort/exit/__assert_fail) cut their block with UNREACHABLE so cold
     assert/abort paths no longer force a frame or callee-saved saves;
     qualifying functions shrink-wrap (frameless hot path, per-cold-block
     prologue). Memory operands: single-use loads fold into add/sub/and/
     or/xor/imul/cmp (incl. cmp in br)/SSE add/sub/mul/div/ucomis when no
     store/call/divide intervenes. AWFY -O2: 5.9s old -> ~4.8s, gcc 4.6s
     (Bounce 107->56ms, CD ~1.0x, Towers 54->31ms via tailrec).
     Remaining rejections: bitfields, inline asm, atomics, volatile,
     complex/decimal/int128/long double, labeled loops (C2Y).
  3.1 DONE a (SROA) and b (frame-slot inlining). NEXT (performance, priority
     order):
     e. NOT NEEDED so far: exit-edge splitting for iv_widen - no loop in
        test_*.c, bench.c or AWFY has an I32 IV used in an exit phi.
     i. FINDING (do not re-chase): AWFY Queens swings 29 <-> 37 ms between
        builds whose placeQueen is byte-identical. Neither 16-byte function
        alignment nor 16/8-byte loop-header alignment (both tried, built,
        measured min-of-3) moved the suite, so it is a microarchitectural
        effect (4K aliasing / front-end window), not layout we can fix.
        Compare AWFY runs by min-of-3 per benchmark and ignore <10% on
        benchmarks under 60 ms.
  3.2 NEXT (eligibility/coverage). MEASURED 2026-11-03 (loop over
     test/test_*.c, rcc -O2 -fdump-ir): 1289 of 1614 functions go through
     the IR = 79.9% (was 77%). Rejection histogram: load type 54, assign
     type 46, inline asm 43, volatile 27, return type 22, bitfield/variable
     member 22, function attribute 13, call without callee 13, special
     callee 11, labeled loop 10, cleanup/VLA 9, unsupported lvalue LT 6,
     argument type 5, ATOMIC_STORE 4. The type reasons are long double /
     _Complex / __int128 / decimal / _Float16 (rare in hot code).
     Neither bench/bench.c nor the AWFY suite has a single rejected
     function or any volatile, so none of these move the benchmark tables;
     they only widen what -O2 accelerates in user code. Priority therefore
     follows real-code payoff, not the coverage number:
     a. 'function attribute' (13) and 'call without callee' (13): cheap
        builder checks, look at which attributes/call shapes (likely
        constructor/aligned/naked and unprototyped calls) are rejectable
        in the IR vs genuinely unsupported.
     d. long double/_Complex via the x87/AST fallback stays (by design);
        inline asm stays a whole-function fallback.
  3.3 REJECTED (recorded so it isn't re-litigated): UB-based IV widening
     of <=-bounded loops (would help Sieve; violates the no-UB-assumptions
     rule the tests depend on). A -fstrict-overflow-style opt-in flag
     could revisit it deliberately.
  5. Win64 IR leftovers: use xmm6-xmm15 (callee-saved there, needs save/restore
     in the prologue); by-pointer aggregates in stack argument positions stay
     on the AST backend (its callee reads them as raw bytes); no cold-block
     frames (the SEH unwind info covers one entry prologue only).
  6. Bench hygiene: re-run bench/bench_report on a quiet machine (current
     -O2/-O3 spreads of 26-70% were taken under agent load) and re-sync;
     track AWFY per-benchmark after 3.1.a.

  Gate every step on `./run_tests "./rcc -O2" --all --parallel --timeout 60`
  (and the default-flags suite; run the two serially, agents may not run
  make or the suites concurrently with each other).

- interchange and extended float types (just aliased, no __STDC_IEC_60559_TYPES__)

- DWARF -g2/-g3: remaining gaps

  Done (ELF x86-64 + libdwarfp, src/dwarf_g3.c, test/test-g3.sh): subprograms,
  parameters, locals, lexical blocks, globals, typedefs, types, bit-fields,
  macros (.debug_macinfo, -g3 only), real section-relative relocations.
  Levels follow gcc: -g/-g1 line table only (default), -g2 full, -g3 full +
  macros; -g2/-g3 elsewhere degrade to -g1.

  Open:
  - Locals: if/switch/while init scopes (only blocks and for-init are
    recorded), block-scope statics, VLAs, TLS (DW_OP_const8u +
    DW_OP_GNU_push_tls_address), register-promoted locals (-O2 vreg: emitted
    without a location; IR backend is already off under -g).
  - Typedef names are not kept on variable/member types (they resolve to the
    underlying type); unused tagged types and enums are not emitted.
  - Frame info: .eh_frame (src/eh_frame.c) exists only for -g builds on ELF
    x86-64, AST backend only. Without -g, and for the SSA IR backend's
    functions (which have their own prologues, including shrink-wrapped
    per-cold-block ones), there is no CFI; extending it needs IR-side events
    plus a -fasynchronous-unwind-tables default. rcc's own helper routines
    (alloca helper, ASan ctors) have no FDE. No CFI for AArch64/Mach-O/COFF.
  - Macros: command-line (-D) and builtin macros are not recorded; include
    line numbers are 0.
  - Other targets: AArch64 ELF, Mach-O, COFF/PE (writers need extra-section
    reloc support for .debug_*, then reuse dwarf_g3.c with arch-specific
    frame base/reloc types).
  - Without libdwarfp there is no built-in -g2/-g3 emitter (documented degrade).
  - libdwarfp quirks worked around: DW_AT_data_bit_offset is rejected
    (DWARF2 bit_offset used), DW_AT_macro_info is never added by the library
    (placeholder attribute patched after dwarf_transform_to_disk_form_a),
    dwarf_def_macro corrupts the stream (.debug_macinfo is written directly),
    dwarf_get_section_bytes_a hands large sections out in several chunks
    (dwarf_g3.c merges them; obj_libdwarf.c only bounds-checks the first
    chunk), drd_length is unreliable.
